  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 357 AM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nA northwest upper level flow will remain over the forecast area\ntoday and through the upcoming work week. Currently, a broad\nupper lever ridge was located over the western U.S. Numerous\nshortwaves were propagating through the ridge, with a couple\ntracking through the Northern Rockies. Another shortwave was \ntracking through the western Canadian Provinces, and finally, a \nstrong wave was dropping southeast through northern Canada \naround a developing Hudson Bay Low. \n\nThe initial shortwave energy may spark isolated to scattered \nthunderstorms over western ND this afternoon. The southwest will\nbecome moderately unstable with SB Capes around 1000-1500 J/kg.\nBulk shear, initially modest does increase late this afternoon \ninto this evening with 30 to 40kts of shear over the area. A \ncouple of forecast soundings in the far southwest indicate a \nmarginally severe to severe atmosphere may be possible. The main\nissue may be timing of shortwave energy lining up with peak \ninstability. Both are present, but if they don't line up well, \nstrong convection may not occur. A look at the latest CAMs shows\nmost do develop some convection over/near the southwest late \nthis afternoon into early this evening. A few do show stronger \nupdrafts, while others are pretty weak. The main hazard would be\nsmall hail. Will continue to monitor, but at this time will not\nmention severe storms. Better chances for strong to severe \nstorms remains over western South Dakota.\n\nLater tonight the aforementioned western Canadian wave and it's\nattendant surface low, tracks across the southern Canadian \nPrairie Provinces. Its surface warm front, which was lingering \nover eastern Montana, and its quickly following cold front \ntracks east across the forecast area tonight. So we have the \nearly convection in the southwest, then later convection \ntracking northeast to southwest across the forecast area. By the\ntime the northern convection makes it into the state, \ninstability will be minimal and strong to severe storms are not \nanticipated. There may not be that much in the way of convection\nat all, with scattered showers and maybe some isolated \nthunderstorms. \n\nFinally, the northern Canadian shortwave diving south, catches \nup with the leading cyclone over southeast North Dakota by \nMonday afternoon. Convection tracking through the area late \ntonight may linger over the south central and into eastern ND \non Monday morning. By the time the northern front catches up and\nhelps to invigorate convection over eastern ND, it is forecast \nto be out of our neck of the woods. \n\nThus after all this, some stronger convection can not be ruled \nout later this afternoon into early this evening over the \nsouthwest. Later this evening and overnight, additional \nisolated to scattered showers track southeast across the \nforecast area, and may linger into Monday morning in the south \ncentral and James River Valley. If convection does re-invigorate \nMonday, it looks like the potential for severe storms will be \nsoutheast of the forecast area.\n\nWe cool down on Tuesday behind the Canadian cold front with\nhighs mainly in the 70s. We then see a warm-up with highs \nmainly in the 80s by Thursday, then mid 70s to mid 80s into the\nweekend. With the northwest upper flow remaining over the area,\ndaily hit and miss thunderstorms chances will remain in the \nforecast but with low predictability in timing and placement.",
